   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public health law and the insurance law, in relation to utilization review of coverage of nursing home care following an inpatient hospital admission",
    "basePrintNo": "S6492",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S6492-2015",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §4903, Pub Health L; amd §4903, Ins L",
    "lawSection": "Public Health Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6492\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the public health law and the\ninsurance law, in relation to utilization review of coverage of\nnursing home care following an inpatient hospital admission\n\n PURPOSE: :\n\nTo decrease delays for post-acute care by adding nursing home care to\nthe types of care for which an insurer must make a determination on\nauthorization within twenty four hours.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS: :\n\nSection one amends paragraphs (a) and (c) of subdivision 3 of section\n4903 of the Public Health Law by requiring that health care plans make\na determination involving continued or extended post acute nursing\nhome care within twenty four hours of receipt of request for services\nand by prohibiting denial of nursing home coverage on the basis of\nmedical necessity or lack of prior authorization while the\ndetermination is pending.\n\nSection two makes the aforementioned amendments to paragraphs 1 and 3\nof subsection (c) of section 4903 of the Insurance Law.\n\nSection three provides that this act shall take effect 90 days after\nenactment.\n\n JUSTIFICATION: :\n\nMany patients require home care or skilled nursing home care upon\ndischarge from the hospital. Hospitals increasingly experience delays\nin obtaining authorizations from insurers for post-acute care. Since\nmost insurers pay hospitals based on diagnosis-related group systems,\nit does not cost the insurer any more to have patients stay in the\nhospital for a few extra days and doing so can reduce insurer costs\nfor post-acute care. This is not good for patients, nor is it good for\nhospitals trying to operate as efficiently as possible.\n\nState law has been modified twice in recent years to tighten\ntimeframes for authorization for post-acute home care and substance\nabuse services. This legislation will extend the tightened timeframe\nto skilled nursing home services, requiring insurers to make\ndeterminations on requests for nursing home care following an\ninpatient admission within 24 hours of receipt of a request as long as\nthe request is made at least 24 hours prior to discharge from the\nhospital. This change is particularly important now that the Medicaid\nnursing home benefit has moved from Medicaid fee-for-service to\nmanaged care.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY: :\n\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS: :\n\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE: :\n\n90 days after enactment.\n\n\n\n\n\n",
